¶
{{smaller block|“Crucifige,” quod a cacchepolle. “I warante hym a ''witche!''{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xviii. 46.}}}}
{{smaller block|There was a man in that citee, whos name was Symount, a ''wicche.''{{right|''Wyclif'', Acts viii. 9.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Devil or devil’s dam, I’ll conjure thee:
Blood will I draw on thee; thou art a ''witch.''</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., i. 5. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|When a Country-wench cannot get her Butter to come, she says, The ''Witch'' is in her Churn.{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 82.}}}}
'''2.''' An old, ugly, and crabbed or malignant woman; a hag; a crone: a term of abuse.
{{smaller block|Foul wrinkled ''witch'', what makest thou in my sight?{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., i. 3. 164.}}}}
'''3.''' A fascinating woman; a woman, especially a young woman or a girl, possessed of peculiar attractions, whether of beauty or of manners; a bewitching or charming young woman or girl. [Colloq.] —'''4.''' A charm or spell. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>If a man but dally by her feet,
He thinks it straight a ''witch'' to charm his daughter.</poem>{{right|''Greene'', George-a-Greene, p. 262. (''Davies.'')}}}}
'''5.''' A petrel: doubtless so called from its incessant flight, often kept up in the dark.—'''6.''' A water-witch.—'''7.''' The pole, pole-dab, or craig-fluke, a kind of flatfish.—{{smaller|'''Black witch.''' Same as ''ani'' (which see, with cut). ''P. H. Gosse.'' [Jamaica.]—'''The riding of the witch.''' See ''riding''<sup>1</sup>.—'''White witch''' or '''wizard,''' a witch or wizard of a beneficent or good-natured disposition.}}
{{smaller block|Sorcerers are too common; cunning men, wizards, and ''white-witches'', as they call them, in every village.{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 271.}}}}
{{smaller block|And, like ''white witches'', mischievously good.{{right|''Dryden'', The Medal, l. 62.}}}}

